# Line endings, declared rather than left to each machine's git config.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default and rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. Harmless for a file nobody reads back; NOT harmless for one this
# software writes to and asserts about. On 3 September 2026, the first time
# this branch was checked out on Windows, `test_the_lf_file_stays_lf` failed
# before a line of code ran -- git had already converted `rate_plans.yaml` on
# the way to disk.
#
# The test was right and the repository was silent. This is the repository
# saying what it needs.
* text=auto

# Config this app edits and writes back. The editor preserves LF; checkout
# must not undo that.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Windows entry points, which keep CRLF to be safe in every shell.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# credit-suite's Python sources, for the same reason as the goldens above. The
# mutation harness matches multi-line anchors against the working tree byte for
# byte, so on a checkout where core.autocrlf rewrote them (Git's Windows
# default) every multi-line mutation fails to find its target and the harness
# cannot run at all. Added 4 Sep 2026 after exactly that happened on the build
# PC: `formula-text-blind: expected 1 occurrence ... found 0`.
credit-suite/**/*.py text eol=lf

# canon's record, for a third instance of the same thing. `canon/release.py`
# hashes CONVICTIONS.md, TENETS.md and two skills so a version says what it
# contains; hashing raw bytes made that digest depend on WHO checked the repo
# out, because nothing here forced LF for Markdown. Same commit, different hash
# on Windows, and the check fails on one platform or the other whichever machine
# wrote it. Caught in review on 4 Sep 2026, before it reached a Windows box.
#
# `release.py` normalises line endings when hashing, so the check is correct
# either way. This is the second belt: the file on disk is the same file
# everywhere, which is what the two incidents above are about.
canon/**/*.md text eol=lf
